[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m typedef enum cb_impairment { cbProtanopia, cbDeuteranopia, cbTritanopia, } cb_impairment;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void colourblind(cb_impairment Impairment, float *R, float *G, float *B);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `switch (Impairment)` in `src/lib.c:25` branches on it, giving **3 valid states**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`0`, `1`, `2`), each selecting a different 3x3 matrix. There are no other flags,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no `#ifdef`s, no environment variables, no init/config struct and no global
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m state. (`grep -c '#ifdef\|#if ' src/lib.c` -> 0.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Axis 2 — public entry points, including the lowest level
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | entry point | linkage | reachable across FFI? |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------------|---------|-----------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `colourblind` | exported (`T` in `nm -D`) | yes — the one-shot dispatcher |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `Protanopia` | `static` | no |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `Deuteranopia` | `static` | no |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `Tritanopia` | `static` | no |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The three matrix routines **are** the low-level entry points, but `static`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m linkage keeps them out of both `.so`s' dynamic symbol tables (verified in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SYMBOLS.md`). They are therefore driven directly and individually by pinning
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Impairment` to `0` / `1` / `2`, which is the only way an external caller can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reach them; every row below names which one it exercises. Rows are not confined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to a single "convenience wrapper" — `colourblind` *is* the whole API.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Axis 3 — input value shapes the code distinguishes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/lib.c` contains no value-dependent branch: each transform is nine
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` instructions, straight-line. The distinctions that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matter are the ones the **hardware** draws, since they are what can diverge
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m between the C and Rust codegen:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | class | why the code/hardware treats it differently |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|---------------------------------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V1 typical colour, each channel in `[0,1]` | the intended domain; ordinary normals |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V2 arbitrary finite normal (full exponent range, random mantissa) | exercises rounding of every product and sum; catches a wrong coefficient in a low mantissa bit |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V3 `+0.0` / `-0.0` | signed-zero rules: `-0.0 + 0.0 == +0.0`, `c * -0.0` sign, and `x - x == +0.0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V4 subnormals incl. `f32::from_bits(1)` | gradual underflow; products of subnormals flush to zero |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V5 `±FLT_MAX`, `±FLT_MIN` | products overflow to `inf` / underflow to subnormal, so the later adds see specials |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V6 `±inf` | `inf * c`, `inf + inf`, and `inf - inf` -> invalid op |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V7 quiet NaN, varying sign and payload | NaN payload/sign propagation; SSE forwards the **`dst`** operand when both are NaN, so operand roles must match |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V8 signalling NaN, varying sign and payload | SSE quiets it (sets mantissa MSB) while preserving sign and the rest of the payload |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V9 mixed — exactly one channel special, the other two ordinary normals (all 3 positions x V3..V8) | the case that actually pins the `dst`/`src` priority per instruction; a uniformly-special input hides it |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | V10 unit basis vectors `(1,0,0)`, `(0,1,0)`, `(0,0,1)` | recovers each matrix coefficient **bit-exactly**, so a mis-rounded literal is caught directly |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Axis 4 — pointer/aliasing shapes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each transform copies all three inputs into locals **before** writing any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m output (`float R = *Red, G = *Green, B = *Blue;`), so aliasing is a real,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m well-defined configuration, and a translation that wrote outputs incrementally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would diverge here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | class | shape |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|-------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A1 | three distinct `float`s |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A2 | `R` and `G` are the same object (`p, p, q`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A3 | `R` and `B` are the same object (`p, q, p`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A4 | `G` and `B` are the same object (`p, q, q`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A5 | all three are the same object (`p, p, p`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | A6 | three distinct, non-contiguous heap allocations (rules out any assumption of adjacency) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Axis 5 — feature combinations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/Cargo.toml` has **no `[features]` table**, so the cross-product over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m features is a single point: the default build. `--all-features`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `--no-default-features` and the default are the same configuration; the harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs all three anyway.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Table
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cross-product of Axes 1 x 3 x 4, pruned to the combinations the code actually
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinguishes. Every row runs **many randomized inputs with a fixed seed**
